<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Caboloan</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1576 CE (approx.) · capital Binalatongan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Pangasinan on the Lingayen Gulf of Luzon, which sent envoys to Ming China and traded deerskins with Japan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Ming records name a polity called Feng-chia-hsi-lan, identified with Pangasinan, which sent tribute missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the early 15th century under a ruler named Kamayin. The people lived around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayen-gulf">Lingayen Gulf</a> and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agno-river">Agno River</a>, with their centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-carlos-pangasinan">Binalatongan</a>, later San Carlos.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Caboloan was governed by a hereditary chief over a society of nobles, freemen and dependants. It exported deerskins, gold and salt, and in the 16th century Japanese traders visited, so that the Spanish called the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agoo">Agoo</a> on the gulf Puerto de Japon. Its people practised indigenous religion.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Spanish forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-salcedo">Juan de Salcedo</a> reached Pangasinan in 1572, and the area was brought under Spanish rule in the following years. Pangasinan became a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a> and its people resisted in revolts such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-malong">Andres Malong</a> in 1660.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/caboloan.jpg" alt="Caboloan" width="320" height="364"><figcaption>Image: Delirium333 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caboloan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1576 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Binalatongan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kamayin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous Philippine religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pangasinan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pangasinan, Feng-chia-hsi-lan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1488&amp;focus=caboloan" aria-label="Caboloan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8051 1760 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Caboloan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7643 1242h0M8309 1890h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8371 2016h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8371 2016h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Philippines. <a href="/atlas?year=1488&amp;focus=caboloan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

